bf88b5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087605 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root $\\lambda = 3,$ the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}-6(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+9\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+12x+2-18{x}^{2}-12x+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =1 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087614 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087614 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\nAe^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = 1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbf37829-4b55-5a39-bf20-d067b34db8fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087614 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{3x}+3Bx{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+3B(0){e}^{0}+0{e}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =3A+B \\\\ 1 & =3(2)+B \\\\ -5 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2{e}^{3x}-5x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087869 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045