] [17592186088391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088391 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088391 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 8\\frac{dy}{dx} + 16y = 6e^{4x}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22d4aef5-fb21-57f9-83fe-23733435808c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088803 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088803 #edb/ref 1061 593872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088803 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02ab851a-75bb-55bf-ac1f-e123bca5af20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088803 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088804 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088804 #edb/ref 1061 593873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088804 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cb38143-b564-54a8-bcbd-0ba84a764e50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088804 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088805 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088805 #edb/ref 1061 593874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088805 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90d04c15-aa41-5e9b-9985-a256cbc67b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088805 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088806 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088806 #edb/ref 1061 593877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088806 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "236677d6-a196-55c3-9d1f-b2c7cf414ef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088806 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088807 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088807 #edb/ref 1061 593878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08b8d2e2-bf6e-5a6b-97ae-b1e8fa3a2ee4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088808 #edb/ref 1061 593881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8641b402-b33c-58ce-8d54-a569e2caa272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088809 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088809 #edb/ref 1061 593882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088809 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "309ded05-b534-5412-a7ef-d8e18e600691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088809 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088810 #edb/ref 1061 593885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088810 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7099023d-1af5-5693-a8f7-552a9070f7be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088810 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088947 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **exponential forcing** if $f(x)$ is an exponential function. For example, the ODE below has exponential forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =10 e ^ {2x}\n$$\n\nThe method for solving a second-order ODE with exponential forcing is similar to the method for solving a second-order ODE with polynomial forcing. The general solution is still the sum of the complementary solution $y_c$ (i.e., the solution to the associated homogeneous ODE) and a particular solution $y_p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nThe only difference is in how we find a particular solution. Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ provided that $e^{bx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{bx}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a known constant and $\\alpha$ is an unknown constant to be determined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088947 #edb/ref 1065 4063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088947 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a08b4b47-ae27-534c-b39b-1696d513b79e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088947 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088948 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ *is* a term in the complementary solution, then $\\alpha e^{bx}$ *cannot* be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{bx}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3 e ^ {-2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +3) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = -2$ or $\\lambda = - 3.$ Consequently, the complimentary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution to the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $3e^{-2x},$ and $e^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}+\\alpha {e}^{-2x}, \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}-4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―}-4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+5({-2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―}+\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+{6\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―} & =3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 3 x e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088948 #edb/ref 1065 4064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088948 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76ee6f3c-a454-54c3-8075-56c1c0a27261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088948 #edb/ref 1067 "The Case When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088949 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ and $xe^{bx}$ are *both* terms in the complementary solution, then neither can be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{bx}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 36y = e ^{6x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda -6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 x} + Bxe^{6 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{6x},$ and $e^{6x}$ and $xe^{6x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =6\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12x\\alpha {e}^{6x}+36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+12\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & =36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+24\\alpha x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\underline {36 \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}}\n+2 \\alpha e ^{6x} +\\underline{ 24 \\alpha x e ^{6x} }-12(\\underline{6 \\alpha x^ 2e ^{6x} }+ \n\\underline {2 \\alpha x e^{6x}})\n+\\underline{36  \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}} = e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2} x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{c} \\\\  & =A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088949 #edb/ref 1065 4065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088949 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "59c57ff3-a1a4-5ed4-be1c-15350d769274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088949 #edb/ref 1067 "Second-Order ODEs With Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088954 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's solve the following second-order ODE with exponential forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =10 e ^ {2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = -3$ or $\\lambda = -2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $10e^{2x},$ and $e^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2 \\alpha e ^{2x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 4  \\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+6\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ 20\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088954 #edb/ref 1065 4138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088954 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf4c356b-2a4a-5811-905f-b7e539bdc8d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088954 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349155 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349155 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349155 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4e164011-eb35-591c-9a55-ea7723edbb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349255 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349255 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349255 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5826b528-62db-509a-8707-670b6578d7fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349256 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349256 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349256 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "583d410d-04f1-5059-ae2d-f61a6fe7e9e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349449 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349449 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349449 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c9ed6bd-defa-5191-afec-ca21413f9a53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349492 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{2x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{e}^{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349492 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349492 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "715013e1-a0ea-59e7-8f0e-1dea7671cf79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349703 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349703 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349703 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a78a1c3-fb64-53f8-b86f-3f77711dc33a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350051 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350051 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350051 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b55e0689-26fc-5eab-9e1b-11dc8407cd13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350173 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350173 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350173 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c2671d11-442e-51d7-a66a-492a211dc05f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350227 #edb/ref 1080 "y=3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350227 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350227 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9fde841-1b91-56d8-87c8-58e7596aaa43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350397 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}+{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350397 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350397 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "defb955e-8b3b-5d59-a201-8980e6bb9ba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350813 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3c8bc506-cf0d-5153-9c60-9111cee0e1cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186350051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350975 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9bdd8ce2-b031-531f-854e-7b74f8227bab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351192 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351192 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351192 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351192 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=21{e}^{3x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351192 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "703bb75c-b102-5bd2-876e-82612d9b3036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351192 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $21{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+7(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})+12(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9+21+12)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ 42\\alpha  & =21 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351214 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351214 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351214 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351214 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=20{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351214 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c1cbd4b-2b58-54b2-8818-d30f2297ce38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351214 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2,$ which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-8+8){e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2P+2Q+10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4$ and $Q=0.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0)+5{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354678 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0280f284-2b43-53d0-a98d-87e7d4fc25cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355478 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355478 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355478 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5c467016-23d4-54ad-ba9c-738b2aa552ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355534 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355534 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355534 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61f2318e-37cc-5d44-b734-